ybe 678 (if I have W6, W7, W8). If I discard B9, then I still have T9. But the chows would be the same. So the same points apply. But keeping T9 as a terminal might add other points. Wait, but if the hand is Pure Straight, all melds are in one suit. T9 is a terminal, but the chows are in W suit. So T9 is in a different suit. Wait, no. The T9 is in the T (Bamboo) suit. The chows are in W (Characters). So having a T9 would make the hand not a Pure Straight. Because Pure Straight requires all chows in the same suit. So if there's a T9, then the hand is not Pure Straight. Therefore, discarding T9 is better because it allows all melds to be in the same suit. \n\nSo, if I discard T9, then the hand can form Pure Straight (16 points). If I keep T9, then the hand has a terminal tile from another suit, which might break the Pure Straight. Therefore, discarding T9 is better. \n\nWhat about discarding B4? B4 is in the B (Dots) suit. If I discard B4, then I still have T9 and B9. But again, those are terminals in other suits. So the hand would have a Dots 4, which is a middle tile. Not part of any chow. So the hand would still need to form chows in W suit. But with T9 and B9, it's not part of the W suit. So the Pure Straight would still be possible if those are not part of the melds. Wait, if I discard B4, then the remaining tiles are W1, W2, W3, W5, W5, W6, W7, W8, T9, B9, F2, F2, J2. So the T9 and B9 are still there. But to form the Pure Straight, I need to have all chows in the same suit. So if I have those terminals, they are not part of the chows. So the hand would still have the four chows in W suit and a pair of F2, plus the T9 and B9. But since those are not part of the melds, they are just extra tiles. Wait, no. The hand must consist of exactly four melds and a pair. So the T9 and B9 would have to be part of the melds. But they are not. Therefore, discarding B4 would not help. \n\nTherefore, the best move is to discard T9, allowing the hand to form Pure Straight (16 points) plus Fully Concealed (4) and All Chows (2), totaling 22 points. \n\nOther possible discards: What if I discard B9? Then I have T9 left. But same issue as before. The T9 is in another suit. So the Pure Straight would be invalid. \n\nDiscarding J2: Then the hand would have a pair of F2. The rest of the tiles would still need to form the chows. But J2 is a dragon, and its discard might not affect the chows. However, keeping J2 doesn't help with the Pure Straight. So discarding J2 is an option, but it's not as good as discarding T9. \n\nDiscarding W8: Then I can't form the 678 chow. So the hand would have 123, 567, and maybe another chow. But without W8, the 678 is not possible. So the hand would have to form other chows. For example, 567 and 567? Not possible. So the points would be lower. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to discard T9. That way, the hand can form the Pure Straight in the W suit, which gives a high score. \n\nSo the answer should be Play T9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding T9 allows forming a Pure Straight in the W suit, maximizing points.  \nReason: Keeping all chows in the same suit (Characters) enables Pure Straight (16 points), Fully Concealed (4 points), and All Chows (2 points), totaling 22 points, which exceeds the minimum 8-point requirement. Discarding T9 removes an out-of-suit terminal, preserving the hand's structure.  \nAnswer: Play T9"}
