d, maybe it's safe to discard. But I have only one W9, so discarding it would mean I can't use it again. But since it's already in a meld, maybe it's okay.\n\nNow, considering the unshown tiles. The unshown W4 count is 4, so there are four W4s left. If I discard a W4, there are three left. If I keep both, there are two left. So maybe keeping both W4s is better for forming a Pung.\n\nAlso, looking at the game history of other players. The previous player has played several J2s and F4s. The player across has played some B tiles. The next player has played F3s and B5. Not sure if that affects my strategy.\n\nAnother angle: my current hand has two chows, and I need two more melds. If I can form two more chows or pungs. Let's see. If I can get W3, then [W1, W2, W3] as a chow. If I can get T5, then [T3, T4, T5] as a chow. That would give me four chows and a pair. Wait, the pair would be... if I have two W4s, that's a pair. So if I have four chows and a pair, that's a valid hand. The pair would be W4x2.\n\nSo the current hand is: [W1, W2, W4x2, W9, T3, T4, F1]. The melds are two chows. If I can get W3 and T5, then I can form two more chows, making four chows and a pair (W4x2). That would be a Full Flush (all same suit?) Wait, no. The chows are in different suits. Wait, the existing chows are in W (Characters), and the new ones would be W and T. So mixed suits. So Full Flush requires all same suit. Therefore, that wouldn't qualify. But maybe another pattern.\n\nIf I have four chows and a pair, that's All Chows, which is worth 2 points. Also, if all the chows are in different suits, it's a Mixed Straight? Wait, Mixed Straight requires three chows in different suits for 1-9. Hmm, maybe not. Alternatively, if I have four chows, but in different suits, it's just All Chows.\n\nBut if I can form two more chows, then I can have four chows and a pair. That would give me All Chows (2 points) and maybe other patterns. But since the suits are mixed, no Full Flush. Also, since the pair